      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hyndman was incorporated in September 1877 and renamed in December that year after B&O Railroad Superintendent E.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyndman%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Hyndman sits in the valley of Wills Creek, which cuts through Savage Mountain and Little Allegheny Mountain before turning south toward Maryland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyndman%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first known settler, Samuel Waters, built a bridge across Wills Creek before 1800, establishing the area’s earliest infrastructure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyndman%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early Hyndman industries included brickmaking and limestone quarrying, reflecting the region’s rich natural resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyndman%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pennsylvania Route 96 traverses Hyndman as Center Street and Pennsylvania Avenue, connecting the borough north to Schellsburg and south to the Maryland line.",
